# Context

## Background
`ohya usage` reports the SQL functions of each `sql` call by name.
`NormalizeFunctions` keeps a name only when it is in
`FunctionCatalogue`. Each other name becomes "other". The SQLite
authorizer reports the LIKE operator as the function "like" (verified
on 2026-11-24 with a Python sqlite3 authorizer probe, SQLite 3.45.1).
The catalogue did not list "like". So the function that causes most
deadline errors was invisible in the usage report.

## Why This Way
This commit comes first. The test `TestUsageRecordsLikeFunction` of the
next commit needs the catalogue entry.

## Gotchas
- The catalogue is in alphabetical order. Keep it so. No test checks
  the order.
- SQLite reports the name in lower case.

